{
  "slug": "401k-loan-rules",
  "metaTitle": "401(k) Loan Rules: Limits, Repayment Terms, and Default",
  "metaDescription": "Learn 401(k) loan rules, borrowing limits up to $50,000, 5-year repayment terms, and tax consequences if a plan loan is treated as a distribution.",
  "h1": "401(k) Loan Rules: Limits, Repayment Terms, and Tax Consequences",
  "cardBlurb": "A 401(k) loan lets you borrow up to $50,000 or half your vested balance if your plan permits it, subject to strict federal repayment schedules and tax penalties upon default.",
  "introText": "A 401(k) loan allows you to borrow money from your own vested retirement balance if your employer's plan permits loans, up to a statutory cap of $50,000 or half of your vested balance. At ModernWallet, we review the federal statutory rules and retirement plan mechanics so you understand the legal limits before taking money out of your account.\n\nUnder federal tax law, any loan from a qualified employer plan is treated as a taxable distribution unless it satisfies specific statutory exceptions under [26 U.S.C. § 72(p)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72). To avoid being treated as a distribution, the borrowing amount cannot exceed federal dollar caps, must follow a level repayment schedule over no more than 5 years, and must be repaid at least quarterly.\n\nIf you need funds and want to compare borrowing methods or evaluate taking a permanent distribution instead, see our guide on [How to Withdraw From a 401(k)](/guides/how-to-withdraw-from-401k/) or test your potential taxes with our [401(k) early withdrawal calculator](/401k-early-withdrawal-calculator/).",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How a 401(k) Loan Works and How Much You Can Borrow",
      "body": "Under [26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(A)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72), your total 401(k) loan balance across all outstanding loans from the plan cannot exceed the lesser of two statutory limits. The first limit is $50,000, reduced by the excess of your highest outstanding loan balance from the plan during the one-year period ending the day before the new loan is made over your outstanding balance on the date the loan is made. The second limit is the greater of one-half of the present value of your nonforfeitable accrued benefit under the plan or $10,000.\n\nYour nonforfeitable accrued benefit represents your vested balance in the plan. When you evaluate how much you can borrow, the formula applies directly to your vested funds and any prior borrowing history over the preceding twelve months. If your employer plan allows loans up to the federal maximum, your borrowing cap depends entirely on that vested balance and whether you carried a loan balance in the prior year.\n\nConsider three illustrations of this statutory formula for a participant with no prior loans:\n\n1. A participant with a $15,000 vested balance: one-half of the balance is $7,500. Because the statutory formula uses the greater of one-half of the vested balance or $10,000, the applicable limit is $10,000. Comparing $10,000 to the $50,000 maximum, the lesser figure is $10,000. The borrowing cap is $10,000.\n\n2. A participant with a $60,000 vested balance: one-half of the vested balance is $30,000. That figure is greater than $10,000. Comparing $30,000 to the $50,000 ceiling, the lesser figure is $30,000. The borrowing cap is $30,000.\n\n3. A participant with a $200,000 vested balance: one-half of the vested balance is $100,000. That figure exceeds $10,000, but federal law caps the maximum borrowing amount at the lesser of that figure or $50,000. The borrowing cap is $50,000."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Repayment Rules and the Principal Residence Exception",
      "body": "Federal law requires a 401(k) loan to be repaid within 5 years under [26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(B)(i)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72). The terms of the loan agreement must require full repayment within that 5-year window from the date the loan is disbursed. A plan cannot extend an ordinary loan term beyond 5 years without causing the loan to lose its statutory exception.\n\nThe only statutory exception to the 5-year repayment limit is a loan used to acquire a dwelling unit that will be used within a reasonable time as your principal residence under [26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(B)(ii)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72). That determination is made at the time the loan is originated. If the borrowed funds are used to acquire a primary home, the loan agreement may permit a repayment period longer than 5 years as specified by your employer's plan rules.\n\nIn addition to the term limit, [26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(C)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72) requires substantially level amortization of the loan over its entire term. Payments must be made not less frequently than quarterly. This means you cannot structure a 401(k) loan with interest-only periods, back-loaded principal reductions, or a balloon payment at maturity. Each scheduled payment must include both principal and interest in substantially equal amounts throughout the schedule."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Happens If a 401(k) Loan Is Not Repaid",
      "body": "Under federal tax law, a plan loan is treated as having been received as a distribution under [26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(1)(A)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72) if it fails to meet the statutory requirements. When you fail to make required quarterly payments or miss the 5-year repayment deadline, the unpaid loan balance becomes a deemed distribution. Once an amount is treated as a distribution, it is subject to federal income tax in the year the default occurs.\n\nIn addition to regular income tax, an early distribution can trigger an extra tax penalty. According to [Internal Revenue Service (IRS) Topic 558](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc558), the law imposes a 10% additional tax on certain early distributions from retirement plans. Early distributions are generally distributions received before age 59½, though exceptions exist as detailed by the IRS. If a 401(k) loan balance is treated as a distribution to a borrower who has not reached age 59½, that borrower can face both income tax and the 10% additional tax on the unpaid amount.\n\nA different rule applies if an unpaid balance becomes a plan loan offset. Under [26 U.S.C. § 402(c)(3)(A)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72), the ordinary rollover window to deposit distributed funds into an eligible retirement plan or Individual Retirement Account (IRA) is 60 days after the distribution is received. However, for a qualified plan loan offset amount, [26 U.S.C. § 402(c)(3)(C)(i)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/402) provides an extended rollover deadline. You can complete a rollover of a qualified plan loan offset amount up to the due date, including tax extensions, for filing your federal income tax return for the tax year in which the amount is treated as distributed from the plan. Whether an offset qualifies requires specific analysis, so ask your plan administrator or a professional tax advisor about your situation."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Plan-Specific Rules and Interest Deductibility Restrictions",
      "body": "Federal statute authorizes employer plans to offer loans, but it does not require any plan to do so. Whether an employer retirement plan permits borrowing at all is decided entirely by the employer's plan document. If your plan does permit loans, the plan document also determines the application fees, ongoing administrative charges, the specific interest rate charged, the maximum number of loans you may have outstanding at one time, and the exact default procedures.\n\nInterest paid on a 401(k) loan goes back into the participant's account along with principal repayments, but federal tax law restricts your ability to deduct that interest on your tax return. Under [26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(3)(A)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72), no tax deduction is allowed for interest paid on a plan loan during any period the borrower is a key employee. In addition, no deduction is allowed if the loan is secured by certain elective deferrals under the plan.\n\nBorrowers who continue contributing to their retirement accounts while repaying a loan should also monitor their annual addition limits. You can review current annual deferral thresholds in our guide to [401(k) Contribution Limits 2026](/guides/401k-contribution-limits-2026/) to verify how your regular payroll contributions interact with plan participation rules."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Comparing a 401(k) Loan to Alternative Financing Options",
      "body": "A 401(k) loan is one method for accessing liquidity, but it requires repaying your own retirement balance under strict federal timelines and risk of taxation upon failure. If you own residential real estate and want to examine an alternative revolving credit line, read our detailed comparison of a [401(k) Loan vs HELOC](/compare/401k-loan-vs-heloc/) to evaluate how secured home equity borrowing functions alongside retirement loans. If your goal in borrowing is restructuring existing high-interest balances, explore our guide on [How to Pay Off Debt](/guides/how-to-pay-off-debt/) to review structured debt reduction methods before committing retirement assets."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Next Steps for Requesting Your Plan Loan Documents",
      "body": "To evaluate a 401(k) loan for your personal finances, contact your employer's plan administrator to request a complete copy of the plan loan policy in writing. Review the document to confirm whether loans are permitted, the exact fees charged to originate and service the loan, the interest rate calculation method, and the plan's specific default timelines. Verify your current vested account balance directly from your latest participant statement before calculating your statutory limit under the 50% or $50,000 rule."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How much can I borrow from my 401(k)?",
      "answer": "Under 26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(A), the maximum you can borrow across all loans from the plan is the lesser of $50,000 (reduced by your highest outstanding loan balance in the prior 12 months minus your balance on the date of the loan) or the greater of $10,000 or 50% of your vested balance. A participant with a $15,000 vested balance can borrow up to $10,000. A participant with $60,000 vested can borrow up to $30,000, and a participant with $200,000 vested is capped at $50,000."
    },
    {
      "question": "How long do I have to repay a 401(k) loan?",
      "answer": "Under 26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(B)(i), you must repay a 401(k) loan within 5 years from the date the loan is made. Repayment must occur through substantially level payments made at least quarterly over the term of the loan under 26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(C). The only statutory exception to the 5-year repayment limit is when loan proceeds are used to acquire a dwelling unit that will be used within a reasonable time as your principal residence."
    },
    {
      "question": "Do I pay taxes on a 401(k) loan?",
      "answer": "You do not pay taxes when you take out a 401(k) loan that satisfies federal limits and repayment rules. However, under 26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(1)(A), if the loan fails to meet statutory rules or falls into default, the unpaid amount is treated as a distribution. That deemed distribution is subject to regular income tax and can also trigger a 10% additional tax under IRS Topic 558 if you are under age 59½."
    },
    {
      "question": "What happens to a 401(k) loan if I leave my job?",
      "answer": "If you leave your employer and your plan requires repayment, an unpaid loan balance may become a plan loan offset. While an ordinary rollover must be completed within 60 days under 26 U.S.C. § 402(c)(3)(A), a qualified plan loan offset amount can be rolled over into an eligible retirement plan or IRA until the tax filing due date (including extensions) for the year of the distribution under 26 U.S.C. § 402(c)(3)(C)(i). If not rolled over, the amount is treated as a taxable distribution."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I borrow from my 401(k) to buy a house?",
      "answer": "Yes, if your employer plan allows it. Under 26 U.S.C. § 72(p)(2)(B)(ii), the standard 5-year repayment limit does not apply to a loan used to acquire a dwelling unit that is to be used within a reasonable time as your principal residence. The borrowing amount remains subject to the federal limits of $50,000 or half of your vested balance, and repayment must still be made in level payments at least quarterly."
    }
